    "stem": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "The jaguar, <i>Panthera onca</i>, is a large cat that lives mainly in South America. The majority of jaguars have light brown fur with black spots, as shown in Fig. 1.1. Some jaguars have completely black fur, as shown in Fig. 1.2.",
        "source": {
          "qp": 2
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "figure",
        "id": "fig1.1",
        "caption": "Fig. 1.1",
        "src": "figures/q1_fig1_1.png",
        "alt": "Photograph of a jaguar with light brown fur and black spots.",
        "source": {
          "qp": 2
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "figure",
        "id": "fig1.2",
        "caption": "Fig. 1.2",
        "src": "figures/q1_fig1_2.png",
        "alt": "Photograph of a jaguar with completely black fur.",
        "source": {
          "qp": 2
        }
      }
    ],
    "parts": [
      {
        "key": "a",
        "label": "(a)",
        "source": {
          "qp": 2
        },
        "stem": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The pigments involved in fur colour are produced as a result of biochemical pathways that take place in cells called melanocytes. These pathways are similar to those that occur in human melanocytes.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 2
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The melanocortin 1 receptor (MC1R) is located on the cell surface membrane of melanocytes and is coded for by the <i>MC1R</i> gene.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 2
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Fig. 1.3 outlines the processes that occur in jaguar melanocytes.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 2
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "figure",
            "id": "fig1.3",
            "caption": "Fig. 1.3",
            "src": "figures/q1_fig1_3.png",
            "alt": "Cell signalling and pigmentation pathways in a jaguar melanocyte, from tyrosine through substance X to phaeomelanin or eumelanin depending on MC1R activation.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 2
            }
          }
        ],
        "parts": [
          {
            "key": "a.i",
            "label": "(i)",
            "type": "short",
            "marks": 1,
            "source": {
              "qp": 3,
              "ms": 5
            },
            "stem": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Name the substance represented by <b>X</b>.",
                "source": {
                  "qp": 3
                }
              }
            ],
            "topics": [
              {
                "ref": "16.2(6)",
                "role": "primary"
              }
            ],
            "markScheme": {
              "marks": 1,
              "instruction": null,
              "guidance": [],
              "points": [
                {
                  "n": 1,
                  "text": "DOPA / dopaquinone / melanin <b>;</b>",
                  "numberedInMs": false
                }
              ],
              "source": {
                "ms": 5
              }
            },
            "er": [
              "er.q1.a.i"
            ],
            "aiSupplement": {
              "explanation": [
                {
                  "text": "The pathway distinguishes a pigment or intermediate from the enzyme that catalyses its production. A gene codes for the enzyme; its protein product changes the amount of pigment formed.",
                  "refs": [
                    "ms:a.i#1",
                    "syllabus:16.2(6)"
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "reasoningChain": [
                "Locate X between tyrosine and the two pigment branches.",
                "Name an accepted pigment-pathway substance."
              ],
              "inferredMistakes": []
            }
          },
          {
            "key": "a.ii",
            "label": "(ii)",
            "type": "short",
            "marks": 1,
            "source": {
              "qp": 3,
              "ms": 5
            },
            "stem": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "When MC1R is activated a second messenger is produced in the cell.",
                "source": {
                  "qp": 3
                }
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Give an example of a second messenger.",
                "source": {
                  "qp": 3
                }
              }
            ],
            "topics": [
              {
                "ref": "14.1(9)",
                "role": "primary"
              }
            ],
            "markScheme": {
              "marks": 1,
              "instruction": null,
              "guidance": [],
              "points": [
                {
                  "n": 1,
                  "text": "cyclic AMP / cAMP <b>; A</b> calcium ions",
                  "numberedInMs": false
                }
              ],
              "source": {
                "ms": 5
              }
            },
            "er": [
              "er.q1.a.ii"
            ],
            "aiSupplement": {
              "explanation": [
                {
                  "text": "A second messenger is inside the responding cell and transmits a signal triggered at the membrane. The syllabus describes this principle using glucagon signalling; this question applies the same distinction to MC1R.",
                  "refs": [
                    "ms:a.ii#1",
                    "syllabus:14.1(9)"
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "reasoningChain": [
                "Distinguish the membrane receptor from an intracellular messenger.",
                "Give an accepted second messenger."
              ],
              "inferredMistakes": []
            }
          },
          {
            "key": "a.iii",
            "label": "(iii)",
            "type": "short",
            "marks": 2,
            "source": {
              "qp": 3,
              "ms": 5
            },
            "stem": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Substance <b>X</b> is also produced in humans, but a mutation of the <i>TYR</i> gene can result in substance <b>X</b> not being produced.",
                "source": {
                  "qp": 3
                }
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Describe the phenotype of a person with this mutation.",
                "source": {
                  "qp": 3
                }
              }
            ],
            "topics": [
              {
                "ref": "16.2(6)",
                "role": "primary"
              }
            ],
            "markScheme": {
              "marks": 2,
              "instruction": "<i>any </i><b><i>two</i></b><i> from: </i>",
              "guidance": [],
              "points": [
                {
                  "n": 1,
                  "text": "albino / albinism <b>;</b>"
                },
                {
                  "n": 2,
                  "text": "no pigment or melanin in skin / pale skin<br><b>or </b><br>white hair <b>; </b> <b>Ignore </b>fair, skin / hair"
                },
                {
                  "n": 3,
                  "text": "red / pink, eyes / irises <b>;</b> <b>A</b> blue"
                },
                {
                  "n": 4,
                  "text": "poor vision  / described <b>or</b> eyes sensitive to light <b>or</b> jerky eye movements <b>;</b> <b>Ignore </b>blindness"
                }
              ],
              "source": {
                "ms": 5
              }
            },
            "er": [
              "er.q1.a.iii"
            ],
            "aiSupplement": {
              "explanation": [
                {
                  "text": "Tyrosinase links the genotype to the pigment phenotype: a change in the enzyme can interrupt production even though the substrate is available. Describe visible or functional traits rather than repeating the name of the mutated gene.",
                  "refs": [
                    "ms:a.iii#1",
                    "ms:a.iii#2",
                    "syllabus:16.2(6)"
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "reasoningChain": [
                "Identify the effect of failed pigment production.",
                "State two relevant phenotypic features."
              ],
              "inferredMistakes": []
            }
          }
        ]
      },
